---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import CaptureThumb from '../../components/CaptureThumb.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';

const providerFlow = `flowchart TB
  VAULT["Example 2-of-3 collaborative vault"] --> K1["Owner key 1<br/>affected COLDCARD seed"]
  VAULT --> K2["Owner key 2<br/>other origin"]
  VAULT --> K3["Provider-held key<br/>origin must be confirmed"]
  K3 --> A{"Was the provider key also<br/>generated by an affected COLDCARD?"}
  A -->|"No"| B["One affected key<br/>below the 2-of-3 threshold"]
  A -->|"Yes"| C["Two affected keys<br/>threshold may be met"]`;
---

<Article
  title="The wider response"
  description="Published guidance from custody providers, the independent technical analyses, the vendor's weekend statements, the community response and the main positions reported during the COLDCARD entropy incident."
  standfirst="What custody providers, vendors and researchers published, separated from commentary about what the incident means, with each publisher's commercial interest stated. The statements themselves are preserved in the record."
  depth="plain"
  kicker="The response"
  next={{
    href: '/risk/',
    label: 'Your risk, by configuration',
    note: 'for action that depends on your own setup rather than on a general position about custody.',
  }}
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      Unchained and Casa published guidance on 31 July: rotate every
      COLDCARD-generated key to one generated on another device, and treat
      multi-vendor custody as a way to keep a single manufacturer's failure
      below the spending threshold. In collaborative custody that count
      includes provider-held keys, so the practical check is to ask the
      provider which device and firmware generated each key it controls. Two
      long-form analyses, from Wizardsardine and Dustin Dettmer, make checkable
      technical assertions and are adjudicated claim by claim on the pages
      where the underlying evidence lives. Wider arguments about custody models
      were also made, and the incident evidence relevant to each is set out in
      the table below. Over the following weekend Coinkite said it had stopped
      shipping devices and destroyed remaining stock carrying the affected
      firmware, and closed Sunday with a statement acknowledging lasting
      damage; those statements, the community help effort, a board departure at
      OpenSats and three unverified claims that warnings came earlier are
      <a href="#vendor-operations">collected further down</a>. Coinkite's
      published terms, and the Ontario consumer
      statutes that bear on their arbitration and class-waiver clauses, are
      registered separately on <a href="/response/legal/">the legal page</a>.
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <h2>Published operational guidance</h2>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="https://x.com/unchained/status/2083036112449163618" rel="noopener nofollow">Unchained</a>, 31 July 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      Unchained recommended rotating every COLDCARD-generated key to one
      generated on another device. It identified two COLDCARD-generated keys in
      its 2-of-3 vault as the most severe case. It also said that one compromised
      key is normally below threshold, while recommending that clients replace it
      as soon as practical.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="1 Aug 2026"
        scope="Unchained's incident guidance and its assessment of 2-of-3 vault exposure"
        source="Unchained's public guidance, preserved as an X capture"
        href="/record/sources/unchained-guidance/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/casa-incident-guidance/">Casa</a> and
      <a href="/record/sources/nneuman-casa-migration-video/">Nick Neuman</a>, 31 July 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      Casa presented multi-key, multi-vendor custody as a way to remove a single
      manufacturer's failure from the spending threshold. Nick Neuman published
      a migration video that starts with the user's current COLDCARD as one key
      in a new Casa multisig, and said moving the assets there eliminates loss
      risk from this vulnerability. That statement depends on the resulting
      policy, the provenance of every other key and whether the affected key is
      below threshold. Casa and Neuman have a commercial interest in the service
      being recommended.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="1 Aug 2026"
        scope="Casa's multi-vendor-custody position and Neuman's migration-process and risk claim"
        source="Casa and Nick Neuman, captured primary X posts; policy conditions and commercial interest stated above"
        href="/record/sources/nneuman-casa-migration-video/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/ledger-not-affected-response/">Ledger</a>, 31 July 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      Ledger reported that its devices are not affected. It attributed that
      conclusion to a certified true-random-number generator inside its Secure
      Element and said each 24-word recovery phrase receives 256 bits of entropy.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="1 Aug 2026"
        scope="Ledger's not-affected statement and its description of its entropy architecture"
        source="Ledger, captured primary X post; implementation not independently assessed here"
        href="/record/sources/ledger-not-affected-response/"
        compact
      />
      This archive has captured the vendor statement but has not independently
      reviewed the relevant Ledger implementation or runtime output. Ledger is a
      competing hardware-wallet vendor.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="https://x.com/Rob1Ham/status/2082896614218203616" rel="noopener nofollow">Rob Hamilton</a>, AnchorWatch
    </div>
    <p>
      Hamilton published preliminary on-chain accounting for the first observed
      wave: 500 transactions, 1,324 inputs and approximately 594.48 BTC over about
      15 minutes.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="1 Aug 2026"
        scope="Hamilton's preliminary transaction, input, value and duration figures"
        source="Hamilton's captured preliminary X post; the separate block-span and amount reconciliation is published on the funds page with its integer-satoshi recheck artefact"
        href="/record/sources/robhamilton-preliminary-sweep/"
        compact
      />
      The exact reconciliation on <a href="/record/funds/">the funds
      page</a> later established four blocks, 960,188 through 960,191. His original
      post described a three-block window, so the archive preserves both the
      preliminary statement and the corrected chain measurement.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="analyses">Independent technical analyses</h2>

  <p>
    Two long-form analyses published on 1 August 2026 go beyond guidance and
    make checkable technical assertions. Both are recorded here with the
    interest of their publisher stated, and both have been adjudicated claim by
    claim on the pages where the underlying evidence lives.
  </p>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/wizardsardine-postmortem/">Wizardsardine</a>, 1 August 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      Kevin Loaec published a post-mortem whose central contribution to this
      record is its enumeration of COLDCARD features that drew their own
      randomness from the affected generator without consulting the user's
      mnemonic. Its consequence, stated in the accompanying
      <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-derived-feature-exposure/">X post</a>, is
      that a user who imported a seed or generated one with sufficient dice can
      still be exposed through those features.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="1 Aug 2026"
        scope="Wizardsardine's post-mortem and its assertion that imported and dice-generated seeds remain exposed through device features"
        source="Kevin Loaec, captured post-mortem and primary X post; competing interest and the author's own caveat disclosed on the blast-radius page"
        href="/record/sources/wizardsardine-postmortem/"
        compact
      />
      Each asserted feature was checked
      against the pinned firmware and libngu clones; the
      <a href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/#wizardsardine">per-claim adjudication
      is on the blast-radius page</a>.
      <Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="That the asserted feature call sites resolve to the affected generator, and the narrowing of the HSM claim"
        source="Call-site review of the pinned COLDCARD firmware and libngu clones, set out row by row on the blast-radius page"
        href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/#wizardsardine"
        compact
      />
    </p>
    <p>
      Wizardsardine sells Liana, a competing wallet product. That interest and
      the author's own caveat about how the post was written are disclosed
      beside <a href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/#wizardsardine">the same
      adjudication</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/btcpp-dettmer-commit-history/">Dustin Dettmer</a>, bitcoin++ Insider Edition, 1 August 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      A commit-history reconstruction of how the predictable path entered the
      tree. Its commit hashes, line counts and quoted source largely reproduce
      against the clones held here; its dating of the defect's introduction does
      not.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="1 Aug 2026"
        contested
        scope="Dettmer's account of which commit introduced the defect"
        source="Dettmer's captured analysis; the archive's own commit check gives a different date and is set out on the developer-response page"
        href="/record/sources/btcpp-dettmer-commit-history/"
        compact
      />
      The <a href="/response/developers/#commit-history">adjudication and
      the commit reconciliation are on the developer-response page</a>, and the
      piece also bears on <a href="/response/ai/">the question of how the defect
      was found</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="provider-keys">Provider-held keys in the threshold count</h2>

  <p>
    In collaborative custody, the relevant count includes every key that can
    satisfy the spending policy, including keys held by a provider. The public
    record held here does not establish how any particular provider generated
    its own key. The appropriate check is therefore factual: ask the provider
    which device and firmware generated each key it controls, and when.
  </p>

  <div class="mermaid-wrap">
    <pre class="mermaid" set:text={providerFlow}></pre>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    An example only. Actual exposure depends on the wallet policy, which keys
    were generated by affected firmware, and any independent passphrase or dice
    entropy.
    <Claim
      basis="derived"
      scope="The threshold outcomes in the hypothetical 2-of-3 policy diagram above"
      source="Threshold arithmetic applied to a hypothetical 2-of-3 policy; no claim about a named provider's key origin"
      compact
    />
  </p>

  <h2>Reported positions about the incident</h2>

  <p>
    Secondary coverage quoted several broader interpretations. They are listed
    here as attributed positions, with the incident evidence that is relevant to
    each one. The incident itself cannot determine which custody model a reader
    should prefer, because the alternatives carry risks that are not measured by
    this theft.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <caption>
          Attributed positions and incident-specific evidence
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            scope="The attributed custody and dice-policy interpretations in the first two rows below"
            source="Public statements by custody providers and researchers, cross-referenced in the evidence record"
            href="/record/analysis/"
            compact
          />
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            contested
            scope="The open-source-security framing in the third row below"
            source="Contrasting public interpretations of source availability in the incident response"
            href="/record/analysis/"
            compact
          />
          <Claim
            basis="derived"
            scope="The conditional first-spend scenario in the final row below"
            source="Threshold arithmetic and transaction-reveal mechanics documented in the migration analysis"
            href="/risk/moving-funds/#threshold-wallets"
            compact
          />
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="wide">Attributed position</th>
            <th scope="col" class="wide">What this incident establishes</th>
            <th scope="col" class="nw">Basis / dispute</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="wide">Some commentators cited the incident as evidence against single-signature hardware-wallet custody.</td>
            <td class="wide">The first-wave transactions are reported, and this archive's own chain check observed them spending single-signature outputs, which describes the spent scripts rather than each owner's wallet configuration. It does not measure the independent operational risks of multisig or third-party custody.</td>
            <td class="nw"><span class="status status--info">Reported</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="wide">Casa CEO Nick Neuman said that asking most users to roll dice is not a workable default.</td>
            <td class="wide">Pure dice generation and mixed dice input are separate paths. Sufficient pure dice entropy avoided dependence on the faulty generator; the archive does not have adoption data.</td>
            <td class="nw"><span class="status status--info">Reported</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="wide">Some coverage presented open-source firmware as creating attacker access to latent bugs.</td>
            <td class="wide">The source was public. The same availability also allowed Block and independent researchers to inspect the path. The discovery method remains unknown.</td>
            <td class="nw"><span class="status status--info">Reported</span> <span class="status status--warn">Contested</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="wide">Some researchers warned that urgent multisig migration can reveal a previously hidden script and enable a competing spend.</td>
            <td class="wide">That condition is technically possible when the attacker controls enough affected keys but lacks the script or descriptor needed to construct the spend.</td>
            <td class="nw"><span class="status status--accent">Derived</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="vendor-operations">The vendor's operational statements, 2 August</h2>

  <p>
    Beyond the advisory revisions tracked on
    <a href="/record/timeline/">the timeline</a>, Coinkite published a series of
    operational statements over the weekend of 2 August. They are the vendor's
    own accounts of its actions, held as captures; none of the described
    operations is observable from here.
  </p>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-shipments-halted/">Coinkite</a>, 2 August 2026, 18:04 UTC
    </div>
    <p>
      Coinkite said it halted shipments as soon as the vulnerability was
      confirmed and destroyed the remaining units carrying affected firmware,
      and that customers whose devices had already shipped were emailed the
      advisory and migration steps. The same post states that SATSCARD,
      OPENDIME and TAPSIGNER use different codebases and are not affected.
      <a href="/record/sources/tftc-shipments-halted/">TFTC repeated the same facts</a>
      later that evening; the vendor post is the primary statement. In a
      <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-disclaimer-clarification/">separate reply</a>
      the same day, Coinkite said the disclaimer added to its store exists so
      previous buyers are aware of the incident.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The vendor's shipments-halted, inventory-destruction and customer-email statements, its not-affected statement for SATSCARD, OPENDIME and TAPSIGNER, and its stated purpose for the store disclaimer"
        source="Coinkite, captured official X posts of 2 Aug 2026; TFTC's secondary report is held separately"
        href="/record/sources/coldcard-shipments-halted/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-shipments-halted" />
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/">Coinkite, "Update, Sunday"</a>, 2 August 2026, 23:00 UTC
    </div>
    <p>
      The vendor closed the weekend with a statement acknowledging that the
      damage done is permanent and that there are hard questions about the
      company. It described direct customer outreach and migration help since
      Friday, thanked community members who helped unpaid through the weekend,
      and committed to continued work with the wider self-custody community.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The Sunday statement's damage acknowledgement, outreach description, community thanks and forward commitment"
        source="Coinkite, captured official X post, 2 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-update-sunday" />
  </div>

  <h3 id="data-retention">The outreach and data-retention exchange</h3>

  <p>
    During its email outreach, Coinkite
    <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-pii-policy/">said</a> that phone numbers
    and customer personal data are deleted, that many customers were therefore
    unreachable by email, and that an earlier post could have been worded
    better.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The vendor's statement on outreach data handling and PII deletion"
      source="Coinkite, captured official X reply, 2 Aug 2026"
      href="/record/sources/coldcard-pii-policy/"
      compact
    />
    Satochip, a competing hardware-wallet vendor,
    <a href="/record/sources/satochip-data-retention-question/">publicly asked</a>
    how customers received store emails if Coinkite deletes customer data after
    90 days, and requested a plain explanation.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Satochip's data-retention question and its 90-day premise"
      source="Satochip, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026; Satochip is a competing hardware-wallet vendor"
      href="/record/sources/satochip-data-retention-question/"
      compact
    />
    Both statements are held as
    captures; no source held here reconciles them, and the 90-day period is the
    question's own premise rather than an established fact. Coinkite's own
    published figure, in its June 2026
    <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-paper-spam/">"Paper spam attempts"</a>
    statement, is deletion of customer data including physical addresses after
    120 days.
  </p>

  <h2 id="community-response">Community response and governance</h2>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/martybent-endorsement-apology/">Marty Bent</a>, 2 August 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      Marty Bent, whose media work had endorsed the COLDCARD, apologised to
      anyone who bought one on his recommendation and described spending the
      weekend helping people move funds to safety.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="Bent's apology to buyers who followed his endorsement and his account of helping people migrate"
        source="Marty Bent, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/martybent-endorsement-apology/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="martybent-endorsement-apology" />
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/pledditor-owner-outreach/">Pledditor</a>, 2 August 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      One individual described searching X for posts from 2021 to 2025 that
      mentioned buying a COLDCARD and replying to each with an alert about the
      exploit, accepting a possible shadow ban as the cost of reaching owners
      who might otherwise never hear of it.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The described individual owner-outreach effort and its accepted moderation risk"
        source="Pledditor, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/pledditor-owner-outreach/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="pledditor-owner-outreach" />
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/opensats-nvk-board-departure/">OpenSats</a>, 2 August 2026, 20:44 UTC
    </div>
    <p>
      OpenSats announced that NVK is stepping down from its board, effective
      immediately, with the board continuing at eight members until a
      replacement is made. The post gives no reason for the departure. Its
      timing places it inside the incident weekend; the post itself does not
      connect the two.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The OpenSats board departure and interim board size; the post states no reason and this record draws no causal link"
        source="OpenSats, captured X statement, 2 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/opensats-nvk-board-departure/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="opensats-nvk-board-departure" />
  </div>

  <h2 id="prior-warnings">Claims of earlier warnings</h2>

  <p>
    Three separate posts assert that warning signs predate the incident.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      captured="3 Aug 2026"
      scope="That each of the three prior-warning claims below was made, by whom and when"
      source="thebtctherapist, a Stacker News thread and Vlad Costea, each preserved as a capture and linked on its card"
      href="/record/sources/btctherapist-prior-drain-report/"
      compact
    />
    Each
    is held as an attributed claim, and inclusion is not endorsement: the
    underlying material is respectively a screenshot, a video this archive has
    not captured, and the authors' own characterisations. None of the claimed
    prior warnings has been verified here.
    <Claim
      basis="unverified"
      scope="The underlying prior warnings, the claimed blocks by the vendor account and the asserted disclosure-handling pattern themselves"
      source="No underlying report, video capture or independent confirmation is held; the claims remain attributed and unchecked"
      compact
    />
  </p>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/btctherapist-prior-drain-report/">thebtctherapist</a>, 2 August 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      Claims that four years earlier someone reported a COLDCARD wallet drained
      after a seed was generated without dice rolls, and that the reporter was then
      blocked by the vendor account, crediting @Zenul_Abidin. The underlying
      report appears only as a screenshot attached to the post, so neither the
      original report nor the claimed block is established from what is held
      here.
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="btctherapist-prior-drain-report" />
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/stackernews-prior-warning-video/">Stacker News thread</a>, 2 August 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      A thread claims that the maintainer of BTCRecover warned about COLDCARD
      entropy two years before the incident in a YouTube video, and that NVK
      had blocked him on X. Both claims are the thread author's; the video
      itself is not captured by this archive, and neither the warning's content
      nor the claimed block is verified here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--blue">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-blue">
      <a href="/record/sources/vladcostea-disclosure-history/">Vlad Costea</a>, 31 July 2026
    </div>
    <p>
      A thread asserting a pattern in Coinkite's handling of past security
      disclosures, including the 2019 researcher disclosure it describes as
      rewarded with merchandise, and a claim that a later researcher disclosed
      a finding to other vendors but not to Coinkite. The characterisations are
      the author's own and are not verified here.
    </p>
    <CaptureThumb id="vladcostea-disclosure-history" />
  </div>

  <Disclosure
    id="precedent"
    title="Earlier predictable-key incidents"
    note="Debian OpenSSL, Android, Randstorm, Milk Sad and Trust Wallet, compared row by row"
  >
    <p>
      Predictable key generation has documented precedents. They help identify
      recurring failure patterns but do not establish this incident's attack
      cost or outcome, and unlike them this defect also reaches
      <a href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/#wizardsardine">device features that
      never touch the user's seed</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table sortable">
          <caption>
            Comparison of earlier predictable-key incidents
            <Claim
              basis="reported"
              captured="1 Aug 2026"
              scope="The dates, failure modes and impact summaries in the precedent table below"
              source="Primary or project disclosure pages for Debian, Android, Randstorm and Milk Sad; Trust Wallet's captured first thread post plus NVD and Milk Sad for the Trust Wallet row; COLDCARD row checked against the source repository clones held here"
              href="/record/sources/milksad-disclosure/"
              compact
            />
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="nw">Incident</th>
              <th scope="col" class="nw">Affected period</th>
              <th scope="col" class="wide">Established mechanism</th>
              <th scope="col" class="wide">Relevant comparison and limit</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong><a href="/record/sources/debian-openssl-dsa-1571/">Debian OpenSSL</a></strong><small>CVE-2008-0166</small></td>
              <td class="nw" data-sort="20060901">Sep 2006 to May 2008</td>
              <td class="wide">A Debian-specific change made OpenSSL's generator predictable. Debian advised recreating affected cryptographic key material from scratch.</td>
              <td class="wide">Like COLDCARD, a code change reduced the key-generation space without changing the caller's expectation of cryptographic randomness. The exact Debian key spaces depended on key type, size and architecture, so a single universal bit count is misleading.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong><a href="/record/sources/android-securerandom-2013/">Android PRNG</a></strong></td>
              <td class="nw" data-sort="20130801">Disclosed Aug 2013</td>
              <td class="wide">Android reported improper initialization of the underlying PRNG for some Java Cryptography Architecture and OpenSSL uses. Bitcoin applications were among the affected software.</td>
              <td class="wide">Both incidents involved a cryptographic-looking interface backed by insufficient initialization. Android's issue also affected per-signature randomness, so it is not a direct model for COLDCARD's seed generation.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong><a href="/record/sources/unciphered-randstorm-disclosure/">Randstorm</a></strong><small>BitcoinJS and derivatives</small></td>
              <td class="nw" data-sort="20110101">Wallets observed from 2011 to 2015</td>
              <td class="wide">Unciphered attributes vulnerable browser-generated wallets to BitcoinJS's use of JSBN <code>SecureRandom()</code> combined with weaknesses in browser <code>Math.random()</code> implementations.</td>
              <td class="wide">Both cases concern long-lived wallet key material generated through a weak random path. Randstorm's attack work varies substantially by browser and date, unlike a single fixed entropy estimate.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong><a href="/record/sources/milksad-disclosure/">Milk Sad</a></strong><small>CVE-2023-39910</small></td>
              <td class="nw" data-sort="20230701">Libbitcoin Explorer 3.x</td>
              <td class="wide"><code>bx seed</code> used MT19937 with at most 32 bits of initial seeding input while allowing callers to request longer output.</td>
              <td class="wide">This is a direct example of long output not adding entropy beyond a small seed space. Researchers attributed approximately 29.65 BTC in one July 2023 cluster and described their wider loss estimate as a lower bound with attribution caveats.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong><a href="/record/sources/trustwallet-wasm-update/">Trust Wallet</a></strong><small><a href="/record/sources/nvd-cve-2023-31290/">CVE-2023-31290</a></small></td>
              <td class="nw" data-sort="20221114">Addresses created 14 to 23 Nov 2022</td>
              <td class="wide">The vulnerable wallet-generation code used MT19937 with a 32-bit seed. The NVD record reports exploitation in December 2022 and March 2023. A Trust Wallet postmortem quoted in the Milk Sad disclosure reported two exploits totalling approximately US$170,000.</td>
              <td class="wide">The 32-bit generator resembles Milk Sad, but its output-consumption pattern produced a different key space. It is separate from Trust Wallet's unrelated 2025 browser-extension supply-chain incident.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong>COLDCARD</strong></td>
              <td class="nw" data-sort="20210301">Mar 2021 to Jul 2026</td>
              <td class="wide">A <code>#ifndef</code> guard treated a macro defined as <code>(0)</code> as present, allowing the linked software generator. Mk4-class boot reseeding then reduced 40 secure-element bytes to one 32-bit value. The affected period starts from the March 2021 change that routed seed generation through that library; the board configuration itself is older, as <a href="/response/developers/#commit-history">the commit reconciliation</a> sets out.</td>
              <td class="wide">Published attack models differ by device class and assumptions. See <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">the entropy model register</a>; there is no single verified <code>2^n</code> figure for all affected devices.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Remediation evidence is incomplete on both sides of the comparison. The
      Milk Sad team reported no substantial remaining Bitcoin balances above
      US$5,000 in the <code>bx</code> 3.x ranges it analysed, while stating that
      its search was partial. No published denominator exists for COLDCARD
      either, so neither the fraction already attacked nor the fraction already
      migrated could be calculated from public sources captured through 1 August
      2026.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="The proportion of affected COLDCARD seeds already attacked or migrated"
        source="No published denominator for affected COLDCARD-generated seeds has been captured"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

